00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:09.760
So a very simple and easy way
to connect to any Google service

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:11.090
is through internet.

00:01:11.090 --> 00:01:14.950
So if you're on prem, you can
get a dedicated internet access

00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:16.540
and connect to Google service.

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:21.340
If you're consuming these
services from VPC or from GCP,

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:25.210
whether it be a GCE
instance or a GKE cluster,

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:27.040
we have by default
public IPs, and you

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:29.320
can connect to them easily.

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:33.070
But there is-- a
lot of companies

00:01:33.070 --> 00:01:35.860
have security and compliance
requirements which

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:39.910
mandates that you do not
use public IPs, right.

00:01:39.910 --> 00:01:42.970
So to meet that mandate,
you have a cloud

00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:46.810
VPN or you buy an interconnect
service from Google,

00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:50.140
such as a dedicated interconnect
or partner interconnect.

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:55.840
On the GCP side, you deploy
VMs without a private IP,

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:59.750
or you create
private GKE clusters.

00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:02.200
So the problem
statement now becomes,

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:06.220
how do you connect to
Google services privately

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:10.600
from on prem, as
well as from GCP.

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:13.390
So the agenda of this
session is to talk

00:02:13.390 --> 00:02:14.890
about whether
different types-- what

00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:18.130
are the different types of
managed services on Google.

00:02:18.130 --> 00:02:20.110
And then, provide
you the solutions

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:23.470
that are available to connect
privately to these services.

00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:26.110
And to round it out,
we'll have Alim talk

00:02:26.110 --> 00:02:28.630
about how they're using
one of these solutions

00:02:28.630 --> 00:02:32.395
to offer cloud volume
product from NetApp on GCP.

00:02:35.390 --> 00:02:39.410
So there are primarily two
types of Google services.

00:02:39.410 --> 00:02:44.330
The first service is hosted
on Google Infrastructure,

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:49.250
and it is front ended by
an internal Google proxy

00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:54.380
from the compute resources
such as a GCE or a GKE cluster.

00:02:54.380 --> 00:03:00.050
Examples of those services
are GCE, Google Cloud storage,

00:03:00.050 --> 00:03:02.030
BigQuery, Cloud Spanner.

00:03:02.030 --> 00:03:04.340
And the solution that
is available to connect

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:09.260
to these services privately is
called Private Google Access.

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:11.300
The second type
of Google service

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:16.880
is the one that is hosted on
the Google Cloud Infrastructure.

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:20.390
And we use VPC
peering as a primitive

00:03:20.390 --> 00:03:23.120
to connect to these services.

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:25.850
So think of these services
as another customer

00:03:25.850 --> 00:03:26.900
on Google Cloud.

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:30.740
But this happens to be a
service that Google provides.

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:33.050
It's hosted by Google.

00:03:33.050 --> 00:03:36.920
Example of such
services are Cloud SQL.

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:39.650
If you're using GKE, then
GKE Private Clusters.

00:03:39.650 --> 00:03:42.560
And the solution to connect
to these services privately

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:46.585
is called Private
Service Access.

00:03:46.585 --> 00:03:47.835
So these are the two services.

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:51.360
So we looked at--

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:55.020
just to recap, we have two
different types of services,

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:56.890
one hosted on Google
Infrastructure,

00:03:56.890 --> 00:03:59.760
the other hosted on Google
Cloud Infrastructure.

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:05.250
And the ones hosted on Google
Cloud infrastructure is

00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:06.960
called Private Service Access.

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:09.810
The one that is hosted on Google
Infrastructure, the solution

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:12.030
is called Private Google Access.

00:04:12.030 --> 00:04:14.580
I'll talk about
Private Google Access.

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:17.399
Olivier and Alim are going
to talk about Private Service

00:04:17.399 --> 00:04:19.230
Access.

00:04:19.230 --> 00:04:21.459
But before we go
into the solutions,

00:04:21.459 --> 00:04:24.780
let's take a step back and get
a good understanding of what

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:28.710
a VPC is in GCP, because
that forms the basic building

00:04:28.710 --> 00:04:30.580
block of all these services.

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:40.260
So in-- a VPC is basically
a customer's network

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:41.580
in Google Cloud.

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:43.000
It's a virtual network.

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:44.820
And it is global in nature.

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:46.650
What global means
is, let's say you

00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:50.670
have a deployment in US West and
another deployment in US East,

00:04:50.670 --> 00:04:54.090
we do not require you to
create two different VPCs.

00:04:54.090 --> 00:04:57.630
If you have a deployment
in US East and US West,

00:04:57.630 --> 00:04:59.490
you do not need
two different VPCs

00:04:59.490 --> 00:05:01.430
if they want to
talk to each other.

00:05:01.430 --> 00:05:04.470
With global VPCs,
these two deployments

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:06.330
can talk to each
other automatically

00:05:06.330 --> 00:05:09.600
without any special
provisioning on your side.

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:11.400
We also offer VPC peering.

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:15.420
So if you have a VPC in
your project, in your org,

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:17.940
you can connect to another
VPC in the same project

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:20.310
or a different
project in your org,

00:05:20.310 --> 00:05:23.580
or you can connect to another
VPC in a totally different org.

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:27.710
So that's what VPC
peering provides.

00:05:27.710 --> 00:05:31.020
Now, let's talk about
Private Google Access.

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:33.150
We saw how internet
was not an option

00:05:33.150 --> 00:05:34.470
to connect to these services.

00:05:34.470 --> 00:05:38.370
So previously, it
was a simpler task

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:41.520
to figure out
where the service--

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:43.020
where the requests
to these services

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:45.900
were coming from, because each
customer gets a unique public

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:47.010
IP.

00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:50.210
With private IP, we need to
understand which customer

00:05:50.210 --> 00:05:53.050
is sending this request.

00:05:53.050 --> 00:05:56.760
This black box to the
customer is a proxy,

00:05:56.760 --> 00:05:58.440
is an internal
Google proxy, which

00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:00.780
we call Google Cloud Front End.

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:02.970
So it's called a
Cloud Front End proxy.

00:06:02.970 --> 00:06:04.740
What it does is,
like a gateway, it

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:09.300
connects the customer's network
to the Google Infrastructure--

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:11.430
the services lying on
Google infrastructure.

00:06:11.430 --> 00:06:15.610
It understands what IPs
does a customer's VPC have

00:06:15.610 --> 00:06:17.860
and makes sure that the
traffic is ordered back to it.

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:23.460
Now that we understand
the glue, let's

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:28.470
see what is the workflow in
setting up Private Google

00:06:28.470 --> 00:06:29.730
Access.

00:06:29.730 --> 00:06:32.280
So the first step
is to advertise

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:37.950
a specific-- a new prefix
and make DNS changes.

00:06:37.950 --> 00:06:40.590
And this is, pretty much,
these other two steps,

00:06:40.590 --> 00:06:43.460
whether you are on prem
or if you're on cloud,

00:06:43.460 --> 00:06:47.770
if you are using from
a GCE or a GKE cluster.

00:06:47.770 --> 00:06:53.170
Let's look at the
set up for on prem.

00:06:53.170 --> 00:06:58.320
So I talked about announcing a
special prefix, a new prefix.

00:06:58.320 --> 00:06:59.800
Why do we need that?

00:06:59.800 --> 00:07:02.100
Well, Google APIs,
Google Services

00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:04.340
already have a
public IP, you know.

00:07:04.340 --> 00:07:06.750
You are routing to them
publicly on internet.

00:07:06.750 --> 00:07:09.570
So we need a special,
or a new, prefix

00:07:09.570 --> 00:07:16.210
that identifies the private
part to these services.

00:07:16.210 --> 00:07:18.810
And this is called
a restricted VIP.

00:07:18.810 --> 00:07:22.230
If you were to do an NS look
up on the restricted VIP,

00:07:22.230 --> 00:07:28.090
you will get a slash 30 block,
which is 199.36.153.4/30.

00:07:28.090 --> 00:07:30.540
Even though it's--
although it's a public IP,

00:07:30.540 --> 00:07:32.670
it is not publicly routable.

00:07:32.670 --> 00:07:33.630
OK.

00:07:33.630 --> 00:07:36.660
So now that we have
this restricted VIP,

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:39.120
you can go on your Cloud
Router, which is something

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:40.620
that you'll be using
if you're doing

00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:44.650
cloud VPN or an interconnect
partner or dedicated.

00:07:44.650 --> 00:07:47.160
Create a custom route
with this slash 30 block

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:51.550
and announce it
over BGP to on prem.

00:07:51.550 --> 00:07:55.860
Now, your on prem
resources have this route.

00:07:55.860 --> 00:07:58.230
But how does the application--

00:07:58.230 --> 00:08:00.960
how does the client
libraries know

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:04.890
that they need to send
traffic to this slash 30?

00:08:04.890 --> 00:08:07.750
We obviously don't want you
to change your applications.

00:08:07.750 --> 00:08:11.910
So the simplest way to achieve
this is through DNS, right.

00:08:11.910 --> 00:08:14.310
And there are three
popular ways of doing this.

00:08:14.310 --> 00:08:17.520
From on prem, you can
change the config map

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:19.470
to resolve start
up googleapis.com

00:08:19.470 --> 00:08:24.810
to restrictedgoogleapis.com
If you are on GCP,

00:08:24.810 --> 00:08:26.880
you can use the
Cloud DNS product

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:32.159
and, specifically, private
zone feature within Cloud DNS

00:08:32.159 --> 00:08:36.870
to resolve the start out
Google API to a CNAME

00:08:36.870 --> 00:08:39.630
to restrictedGoogleapis.com.

00:08:39.630 --> 00:08:43.260
And also you can use, on
GCP, BIND or any other DNS

00:08:43.260 --> 00:08:43.919
of your choice.

00:08:46.760 --> 00:08:49.750
Now, if you're setting
up private connectivity

00:08:49.750 --> 00:08:52.900
from VPC resources--

00:08:52.900 --> 00:08:57.640
compute resources on VPC,
such as a GC or a GKE cluster,

00:08:57.640 --> 00:09:00.610
you need to select the subnet--

00:09:00.610 --> 00:09:02.650
there is a flag called
Private Google Access,

00:09:02.650 --> 00:09:04.250
so you need to select that.

00:09:04.250 --> 00:09:06.700
And then, once
you create the VM,

00:09:06.700 --> 00:09:10.120
you ensure that
the VM is created

00:09:10.120 --> 00:09:12.610
on this particular subnet.

00:09:12.610 --> 00:09:17.740
Please keep in mind that
the VPC has a routing table.

00:09:17.740 --> 00:09:19.720
And the default route
of that routing table

00:09:19.720 --> 00:09:22.040
still says internet gateway.

00:09:22.040 --> 00:09:23.800
So when you send
out-- with this setup,

00:09:23.800 --> 00:09:27.310
when you send out a
request to a googleapi.com

00:09:27.310 --> 00:09:32.150
and start a googleapi.com,
it uses the default route.

00:09:32.150 --> 00:09:34.090
But even though it says
the internet gateway,

00:09:34.090 --> 00:09:35.860
it's not actually--

00:09:35.860 --> 00:09:39.850
the traffic still stays
on Google backbone.

00:09:39.850 --> 00:09:45.070
The other step is the--

00:09:45.070 --> 00:09:46.840
so there is also,
like, I just talked

00:09:46.840 --> 00:09:48.923
about how there is a routing
table and the default

00:09:48.923 --> 00:09:50.752
out points to internet gateway.

00:09:50.752 --> 00:09:52.210
From a security
perspective, if you

00:09:52.210 --> 00:09:55.293
want to lock down your
network, and if you

00:09:55.293 --> 00:09:56.710
want to remove
this default route,

00:09:56.710 --> 00:09:58.250
you can go ahead and do that.

00:09:58.250 --> 00:10:00.430
And you can have the same 199.

00:10:00.430 --> 00:10:00.930
36.

00:10:00.930 --> 00:10:01.430
153.

00:10:01.430 --> 00:10:04.385
4 slash 30 restricted VIP.

00:10:04.385 --> 00:10:06.010
You can create a
static route with that

00:10:06.010 --> 00:10:09.040
pointing to the default
internet gateway.

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:11.772
I'll reiterate, even though it
says default internet gateway,

00:10:11.772 --> 00:10:13.480
the traffic is not
going out to internet.

00:10:13.480 --> 00:10:15.580
It stays within Google backbone.

00:10:15.580 --> 00:10:19.510
And any traffic in Google that
is destined towards the slash

00:10:19.510 --> 00:10:21.910
30 restricted VIP,
Our [? SDLA ?]

00:10:21.910 --> 00:10:25.120
is smart enough to realize
that this traffic is

00:10:25.120 --> 00:10:30.155
destined to a service behind
the Google proxy, right.

00:10:30.155 --> 00:10:31.780
So these are some of
the steps that you

00:10:31.780 --> 00:10:35.920
can take to connect to services
privately using Private Google

00:10:35.920 --> 00:10:37.030
Access.

00:10:37.030 --> 00:10:38.920
With that, I will hand
it over to Olivier.

00:10:42.189 --> 00:10:44.530
OLIVIER VAUTRIN: Thank you.

00:10:44.530 --> 00:10:45.880
Can you hear me?

00:10:45.880 --> 00:10:46.810
Yes.

00:10:46.810 --> 00:10:48.190
Perfect.

00:10:48.190 --> 00:10:50.080
So thanks, Mahesh.

00:10:50.080 --> 00:10:53.830
So we just looked at how do
we connect services that are

00:10:53.830 --> 00:10:55.420
on the Google Infrastructure.

00:10:55.420 --> 00:10:59.780
So services, such as Just
Yes, Spanner, and so on.

00:10:59.780 --> 00:11:01.030
So we are now looking--

00:11:01.030 --> 00:11:03.880
now going to look at
the services that are

00:11:03.880 --> 00:11:09.180
hosted inside the Google Cloud
Infrastructure, inside GCP.

00:11:09.180 --> 00:11:11.890
And some [INAUDIBLE]
so we are going

00:11:11.890 --> 00:11:16.390
to the point of the segment is,
how can I access from my VPC

00:11:16.390 --> 00:11:20.140
from a service in a VPC
to this marriage service

00:11:20.140 --> 00:11:23.890
privately with a private
IP, and, also, how can

00:11:23.890 --> 00:11:25.900
I do that from on prem.

00:11:25.900 --> 00:11:28.090
So on prem to my
managed service,

00:11:28.090 --> 00:11:30.820
without going through internet.

00:11:30.820 --> 00:11:35.220
So services on the
GCP infrastructure

00:11:35.220 --> 00:11:39.400
are services like Cloud SQL,
Cloud Filer, GKE Private

00:11:39.400 --> 00:11:41.630
Master, and so on.

00:11:41.630 --> 00:11:44.500
So who is using Cloud SQL here?

00:11:44.500 --> 00:11:48.030
Who has already
used Cloud SQL here?

00:11:48.030 --> 00:11:48.670
OK.

00:11:48.670 --> 00:11:51.770
Who has used Cloud SQL
with a private IP, already?

00:11:54.040 --> 00:11:54.540
OK.

00:11:54.540 --> 00:11:56.600
So I see a lot of
hand for Cloud SQL

00:11:56.600 --> 00:11:58.760
but not Cloud SQL
with a private IP.

00:11:58.760 --> 00:12:00.710
So Cloud SQL with
a private IP is

00:12:00.710 --> 00:12:02.210
a service that
has been launched,

00:12:02.210 --> 00:12:05.360
I think it was
back in September.

00:12:05.360 --> 00:12:08.900
And it's using the
functionality that I'm

00:12:08.900 --> 00:12:12.550
going to describe right now.

00:12:12.550 --> 00:12:13.490
OK.

00:12:13.490 --> 00:12:18.020
So services on top of GCP are
using the same building block

00:12:18.020 --> 00:12:24.410
that any other service on GCP,
that we are using VPC, VM,

00:12:24.410 --> 00:12:26.100
and so on.

00:12:26.100 --> 00:12:27.890
So you have on
the left side, you

00:12:27.890 --> 00:12:31.610
have a consumer of
VPC, so a consumer

00:12:31.610 --> 00:12:33.860
that would like to
use the service.

00:12:33.860 --> 00:12:36.140
And on the right side,
we have the producer

00:12:36.140 --> 00:12:39.500
of VPC, the producer that has
developed the service, that

00:12:39.500 --> 00:12:42.120
is managing the service.

00:12:42.120 --> 00:12:46.340
How do we connect to
VCPs together on GCP?

00:12:46.340 --> 00:12:48.890
We are using VPC peering.

00:12:48.890 --> 00:12:51.110
And that's a way we
connect privately

00:12:51.110 --> 00:12:55.730
from a consumer to
a private producer.

00:12:55.730 --> 00:12:58.400
The advantage of VPC
peering is, because there's

00:12:58.400 --> 00:13:02.210
no proxy and no device
between the two VPCs,

00:13:02.210 --> 00:13:05.150
there's direct connection
between the two VPCs.

00:13:05.150 --> 00:13:08.450
It's high output
and very low delay,

00:13:08.450 --> 00:13:11.390
which is very important for
database type of services.

00:13:13.930 --> 00:13:17.080
At the same time,
the producer of VPC

00:13:17.080 --> 00:13:20.290
is going to be unique
for each consumer of VPC.

00:13:20.290 --> 00:13:23.810
So every time a consumer will
like to create a new service,

00:13:23.810 --> 00:13:28.390
we are going to have a specific
producer VPC for the service

00:13:28.390 --> 00:13:31.480
to have a security between
all those instances.

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:40.830
Before explaining how to use VPC
peering for the on prem going

00:13:40.830 --> 00:13:45.090
activity, and so on, I
need to take a quick segue

00:13:45.090 --> 00:13:48.270
and explain a new feature
that we launched for Next,

00:13:48.270 --> 00:13:52.410
and this feature is the
capacity to exchange our custom

00:13:52.410 --> 00:13:55.170
routes with VPC peering.

00:13:55.170 --> 00:13:56.700
And you're going
to see later how

00:13:56.700 --> 00:14:00.367
we are using this feature
for on prem connectivity.

00:14:03.110 --> 00:14:05.880
In the VPC, We have
three types of routes.

00:14:05.880 --> 00:14:08.160
We have subnet routes.

00:14:08.160 --> 00:14:09.930
We have static routes.

00:14:09.930 --> 00:14:13.530
And we have dynamic,
or BGP, routes.

00:14:13.530 --> 00:14:16.830
Subnet routes are the routes
that are already automatic,

00:14:16.830 --> 00:14:18.480
created by the system.

00:14:18.480 --> 00:14:21.120
Static and BGP routes
are routes that

00:14:21.120 --> 00:14:24.240
are created by the
user of the VPC, right.

00:14:24.240 --> 00:14:28.380
And that's why we call static
and dynamic routes custom

00:14:28.380 --> 00:14:31.350
routes, because you
can customize them.

00:14:31.350 --> 00:14:35.580
By default, with VPC
peering, only subnet routes

00:14:35.580 --> 00:14:37.500
are exchanged.

00:14:37.500 --> 00:14:39.290
So by default, when
you do VPC peering,

00:14:39.290 --> 00:14:44.580
all the subnets are exchanged,
but the static and BGP routes

00:14:44.580 --> 00:14:46.440
are not exchanged.

00:14:46.440 --> 00:14:52.440
So to allow the exchange of
static and dynamic routes,

00:14:52.440 --> 00:14:56.970
we have flags that you can
update on the VPC peering.

00:14:56.970 --> 00:14:59.790
So we have import
and export flags

00:14:59.790 --> 00:15:03.050
on each side of the
VPC peering connection.

00:15:03.050 --> 00:15:08.130
So that means four flags,
two on the first VPC

00:15:08.130 --> 00:15:10.730
and two on the second VPC.

00:15:10.730 --> 00:15:14.290
An import and export
flag on each side.

00:15:14.290 --> 00:15:17.910
If you have an export flag
for custom route as true,

00:15:17.910 --> 00:15:20.280
that means that
you are OK to send

00:15:20.280 --> 00:15:22.830
those routes to your next VPCs.

00:15:22.830 --> 00:15:26.370
If you set the flag
import as true, let

00:15:26.370 --> 00:15:31.245
means that you are OK to learn
the routes from the others VPC.

00:15:31.245 --> 00:15:34.470
For those of you that
are familiar with BGP,

00:15:34.470 --> 00:15:36.185
that should be very
familiar to you.

00:15:36.185 --> 00:15:37.560
It's very similar
to policy based

00:15:37.560 --> 00:15:42.410
routing on typical routers.

00:15:42.410 --> 00:15:45.700
Let's take an example.

00:15:45.700 --> 00:15:48.930
So in this example,
I have two VPCs.

00:15:48.930 --> 00:15:54.030
And my goal here, let's assume
that my goal is to create

00:15:54.030 --> 00:15:57.600
routes for specific
subnets I would

00:15:57.600 --> 00:16:00.910
like to send traffic to the VM
B that is on the right side,

00:16:00.910 --> 00:16:01.410
right.

00:16:01.410 --> 00:16:05.400
So I have my VM A for
specific destination

00:16:05.400 --> 00:16:10.210
I would like to have, as a next
stop, VM B on the right side.

00:16:10.210 --> 00:16:14.670
On GCP today, it is not possible
to create a static routes

00:16:14.670 --> 00:16:17.830
with a next stop on another VCP.

00:16:17.830 --> 00:16:20.790
So how do we do that without
creating a static route

00:16:20.790 --> 00:16:24.330
with an export with a
next stop in another VPC?

00:16:24.330 --> 00:16:31.470
So the first thing is we
need to create static routes

00:16:31.470 --> 00:16:33.900
on the second VPC.

00:16:33.900 --> 00:16:34.860
OK.

00:16:34.860 --> 00:16:37.680
So let's look at the routing
table for one minute.

00:16:37.680 --> 00:16:40.980
So on each VPC, here,
we have a subnet.

00:16:40.980 --> 00:16:44.910
That's why we have a local route
that the subnet routes, and we

00:16:44.910 --> 00:16:48.630
have the routes to the subnet
on the other VPC, right,

00:16:48.630 --> 00:16:50.400
with next stop B.

00:16:50.400 --> 00:16:52.980
So 10.30 is my local subnet.

00:16:52.980 --> 00:16:59.160
10.40 is my subnet on VPC B.
Now that I configures the static

00:16:59.160 --> 00:17:03.570
routes on the VPC B, I see a
third route on the right side.

00:17:03.570 --> 00:17:08.280
I see a third route, which is
my static route with 10.100

00:17:08.280 --> 00:17:12.210
sending traffic as
a next stop VM B.

00:17:12.210 --> 00:17:15.930
But still it's not
enough, because on VPC A

00:17:15.930 --> 00:17:18.310
I can still not see the routes.

00:17:18.310 --> 00:17:23.160
So the next step is to configure
an export policy on VPC B,

00:17:23.160 --> 00:17:29.220
so that I can say, please
export that route to VPC A.

00:17:29.220 --> 00:17:30.660
But you can see,
it is not enough,

00:17:30.660 --> 00:17:35.880
because even with
the export I don't

00:17:35.880 --> 00:17:40.140
see the routes on the
routing table of VPC A.

00:17:40.140 --> 00:17:42.910
So what is missing is
an import statement.

00:17:42.910 --> 00:17:45.210
So an import flag as true.

00:17:45.210 --> 00:17:47.790
If I put the import flag
as true, then suddenly

00:17:47.790 --> 00:17:52.860
I see the static route
on my routing table.

00:17:52.860 --> 00:17:53.760
OK.

00:17:53.760 --> 00:17:58.380
So you can see that I need
to configure both flags

00:17:58.380 --> 00:17:59.700
for security reasons.

00:17:59.700 --> 00:18:02.670
So both VPCs need to
agree on what to exchange.

00:18:02.670 --> 00:18:05.070
And once that done,
I'm exchanging

00:18:05.070 --> 00:18:07.440
my static routes or my BGP
routes between my VPCs.

00:18:10.820 --> 00:18:14.540
Another use case
for the exchange

00:18:14.540 --> 00:18:19.880
of static and dynamic routes
is what we call Transit VPC.

00:18:19.880 --> 00:18:21.860
So with Transit VPC,
let's say that I

00:18:21.860 --> 00:18:25.920
would like to share a
VPN or an interconnect

00:18:25.920 --> 00:18:27.890
across multiple VPCs.

00:18:27.890 --> 00:18:30.170
I mean, obviously, a
simple way to do that would

00:18:30.170 --> 00:18:33.250
be to have multiple VPNs.

00:18:33.250 --> 00:18:36.420
But that would be very
complex or expensive.

00:18:36.420 --> 00:18:39.080
So let's say that I
would like to share a VPN

00:18:39.080 --> 00:18:41.370
or interconnect
across multiple VPCs.

00:18:41.370 --> 00:18:43.250
So a way to do that is to have--

00:18:43.250 --> 00:18:47.780
to create a VPC that we
will call a Transit VPC.

00:18:47.780 --> 00:18:51.080
This VPC can be empty
or with resources in it,

00:18:51.080 --> 00:18:54.170
and in this VPC I
am going to have

00:18:54.170 --> 00:18:57.410
export flags on all those VPCs.

00:18:57.410 --> 00:19:00.020
And automatically,
all my routes are

00:19:00.020 --> 00:19:04.070
going to be exchanged between
on prem to all the VPCs here.

00:19:04.070 --> 00:19:05.720
So it's dynamic.

00:19:05.720 --> 00:19:10.190
So even if you change routes
on prem, automatically with BGP

00:19:10.190 --> 00:19:12.950
and with my export
policy, all the VPCs

00:19:12.950 --> 00:19:16.550
will be updated
with the new routes.

00:19:16.550 --> 00:19:18.810
Some advantages
of this solution--

00:19:18.810 --> 00:19:21.290
so there's no bandwidth limits.

00:19:21.290 --> 00:19:24.220
It's VPC peering,
so with VPC peering

00:19:24.220 --> 00:19:26.900
there is no bandwidth limit.

00:19:26.900 --> 00:19:28.726
Its direct connection.

00:19:32.300 --> 00:19:37.700
VPC peering is free,
besides the price of egress

00:19:37.700 --> 00:19:40.590
of no more traffic usage.

00:19:40.590 --> 00:19:43.580
So VPC peering is free.

00:19:43.580 --> 00:19:47.160
And every type of
connectivity is supported.

00:19:47.160 --> 00:19:50.032
So it's VPN, but it
also interconnects.

00:19:53.650 --> 00:19:54.670
OK.

00:19:54.670 --> 00:19:57.850
Now that we looked
at this new feature,

00:19:57.850 --> 00:20:00.610
how to exchange routes between
all the types of routes

00:20:00.610 --> 00:20:06.040
between my VPC, how can I do
that for my prem statements?

00:20:06.040 --> 00:20:09.160
And my prem statements
is, again, how can

00:20:09.160 --> 00:20:13.810
I connect from my on prem
to my managed services?

00:20:13.810 --> 00:20:16.510
And for-- to
connect from on prem

00:20:16.510 --> 00:20:18.960
to my managed service privately.

00:20:18.960 --> 00:20:23.270
I need two things, I
need to exchange routes

00:20:23.270 --> 00:20:25.300
between on prem and the VPC--

00:20:25.300 --> 00:20:28.360
my VPC, the consumer
VPC, and I need

00:20:28.360 --> 00:20:31.360
to exchange all those
routes between the consumer

00:20:31.360 --> 00:20:34.070
VPC and the managed service.

00:20:34.070 --> 00:20:34.570
OK.

00:20:34.570 --> 00:20:35.737
So let's do that one by one.

00:20:38.210 --> 00:20:43.030
First, I have a VPC peering
between the managed service

00:20:43.030 --> 00:20:46.200
and the consumer
of this service.

00:20:46.200 --> 00:20:53.560
I'm exporting all the routes
to the managed service VPC.

00:20:53.560 --> 00:20:58.540
And then I need to configure
a customer advertisements

00:20:58.540 --> 00:21:01.490
on the cloud router
of the consumer VPC

00:21:01.490 --> 00:21:03.680
to send those routes to on prem.

00:21:03.680 --> 00:21:07.330
So with those 2-step
export of routes

00:21:07.330 --> 00:21:12.460
to the managed service VPC
and the BGP advertisements

00:21:12.460 --> 00:21:13.380
to on prem.

00:21:13.380 --> 00:21:17.200
I have full connectivity
end to end between the on

00:21:17.200 --> 00:21:21.910
prem and my managed service.

00:21:21.910 --> 00:21:24.040
OK.

00:21:24.040 --> 00:21:25.990
But that could be a
bit complex, right.

00:21:25.990 --> 00:21:28.450
I mean, so let's say--
let's assume that I just

00:21:28.450 --> 00:21:32.350
want to have a Cloud SQL
instance, or, let's say,

00:21:32.350 --> 00:21:35.900
I want to have a
simple MySQL database.

00:21:35.900 --> 00:21:38.140
Now, suddenly, I need to
understand VPC peering.

00:21:38.140 --> 00:21:40.416
I need to understand
export-import.

00:21:40.416 --> 00:21:42.380
It might be a bit complex.

00:21:42.380 --> 00:21:45.340
So how can we simplify all this?

00:21:47.960 --> 00:21:53.620
And the way we simplify all
this is by creating a new scheme

00:21:53.620 --> 00:21:58.360
called Private Service
Success, now in GA.

00:21:58.360 --> 00:22:02.470
And the way to think about
Private Service Access

00:22:02.470 --> 00:22:07.580
is it's a managed VPC
peering connection.

00:22:07.580 --> 00:22:11.920
So when I want to
create a simple service,

00:22:11.920 --> 00:22:15.670
like a MySQL instance,
the VPC peering connection

00:22:15.670 --> 00:22:18.310
will be automatically
created for me, right.

00:22:18.310 --> 00:22:20.000
All the steps will
be done for me.

00:22:20.000 --> 00:22:24.180
So I don't need to
understand VPC peering

00:22:24.180 --> 00:22:26.058
for a simple service connection.

00:22:29.270 --> 00:22:33.300
So how does that
work in details?

00:22:33.300 --> 00:22:38.020
Let us assume that I am
the owner of the VPC,

00:22:38.020 --> 00:22:41.510
so here is a VPC green.

00:22:41.510 --> 00:22:44.620
So let's assume that I'm
the owner of the VPC green,

00:22:44.620 --> 00:22:48.660
and I would like to
create MySQL instance.

00:22:48.660 --> 00:22:55.210
My first step is to decide which
IP range my service should use.

00:22:55.210 --> 00:23:00.660
So it's to allocate a range
for all my managed services.

00:23:00.660 --> 00:23:05.730
This wrench can be, slash 24,
can be as small as a slash 24,

00:23:05.730 --> 00:23:07.200
or it can be
larger, because-- it

00:23:07.200 --> 00:23:12.220
can be larger because it will be
shared across all my services.

00:23:12.220 --> 00:23:15.840
So let's say I would like
to have 50, 100 services,

00:23:15.840 --> 00:23:17.520
I can have a large range.

00:23:17.520 --> 00:23:21.610
And each one of them can pick
a different IP in this range.

00:23:21.610 --> 00:23:25.350
So we do advise to use a
large range, if possible,

00:23:25.350 --> 00:23:27.450
that many customers
cannot have large range,

00:23:27.450 --> 00:23:32.040
because they don't have a
lot of private IP available.

00:23:32.040 --> 00:23:35.490
But if you can, it's better to
have a large range like a slash

00:23:35.490 --> 00:23:38.520
16, and then every
time you create

00:23:38.520 --> 00:23:43.270
a new service they can pick
addresses from this range.

00:23:43.270 --> 00:23:48.100
So in this example, I'm
the owner of VPC green.

00:23:48.100 --> 00:23:51.930
I located a slash 24.

00:23:51.930 --> 00:23:56.550
On the right side, you can
see the UI of the Cloud SQL

00:23:56.550 --> 00:23:58.200
creation.

00:23:58.200 --> 00:24:02.160
And it's as simple as
clicking on private IP instead

00:24:02.160 --> 00:24:03.010
of public IP.

00:24:03.010 --> 00:24:10.070
So if you go to Advanced
Option on MySQL, on Cloud SQL,

00:24:10.070 --> 00:24:12.900
there's an option on
advanced networking

00:24:12.900 --> 00:24:14.820
to select private IP.

00:24:14.820 --> 00:24:19.410
So when you select
private IP, it connects.

00:24:19.410 --> 00:24:21.930
Automatically your
system is going to pick--

00:24:21.930 --> 00:24:30.000
to create a MySQL instance in a
VPC using the rights IP range,

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:34.350
using a private IP in
the ranges you selected.

00:24:34.350 --> 00:24:37.290
And we automatically create
the VPC peering for you.

00:24:44.440 --> 00:24:47.482
Now that we gave an
example for one service,

00:24:47.482 --> 00:24:50.065
let's take an example with two
services, or multiple services.

00:24:53.350 --> 00:25:00.160
In this example, again I am
the owner of the green VPC,

00:25:00.160 --> 00:25:03.460
and I would like two
services, instead of one.

00:25:03.460 --> 00:25:04.480
Same way.

00:25:04.480 --> 00:25:07.240
I can go in the UI
and say, I would

00:25:07.240 --> 00:25:12.760
like to have a service
from NetApp or from Google.

00:25:12.760 --> 00:25:15.220
And the system is going
to create, automatically,

00:25:15.220 --> 00:25:19.670
one VPC peering per
producer of those services.

00:25:19.670 --> 00:25:21.460
So in this case, if,
let's says, there's

00:25:21.460 --> 00:25:24.730
two producers,
Google and NetApp.

00:25:24.730 --> 00:25:27.760
I'm going to have a VPC
peering for each one of them.

00:25:35.310 --> 00:25:37.790
Share VPC support.

00:25:37.790 --> 00:25:44.360
So the issue with shared
VPC is, in this case,

00:25:44.360 --> 00:25:47.810
there's two service
projects in my shared VPC.

00:25:47.810 --> 00:25:52.430
So with shared VPC, I can divide
my VPC in multiple service

00:25:52.430 --> 00:25:55.430
projects so that each team
can have different billing

00:25:55.430 --> 00:25:59.275
and different item rights
for the resources in my VPC.

00:26:03.270 --> 00:26:05.330
The issue with shared
VPC in this case

00:26:05.330 --> 00:26:07.910
is typically the owner
of a service project

00:26:07.910 --> 00:26:13.200
do not have permission to
create the VPC peering.

00:26:13.200 --> 00:26:17.450
And so in theory they cannot--

00:26:17.450 --> 00:26:20.303
the owner of this project
cannot create a connection

00:26:20.303 --> 00:26:22.220
to a service, because
it will have permission,

00:26:22.220 --> 00:26:24.770
and they need to ask you for
every creation of service.

00:26:24.770 --> 00:26:27.520
They need to ask the permission
from the network admin--

00:26:27.520 --> 00:26:30.980
from network admin
owner of the shared VPC.

00:26:30.980 --> 00:26:33.260
So the way we
solve that is, yes,

00:26:33.260 --> 00:26:36.900
for the first connection
to a service provider,

00:26:36.900 --> 00:26:39.920
you do need to ask
for permission.

00:26:39.920 --> 00:26:43.640
But for every other
connection, the VPC peering

00:26:43.640 --> 00:26:44.940
is already there.

00:26:44.940 --> 00:26:48.120
And so you don't need
to ask for permission.

00:26:48.120 --> 00:26:53.700
So the permission is asked once
for every service provider.

00:26:53.700 --> 00:26:55.760
And when the service provider--

00:26:55.760 --> 00:26:57.950
when the connection has
been created to the service

00:26:57.950 --> 00:27:03.620
provider, each owner of those
services-- of those service

00:27:03.620 --> 00:27:07.020
projects can create instances
without asking for permission.

00:27:10.940 --> 00:27:15.500
To summarize, Mahesh discussed
about all the services running

00:27:15.500 --> 00:27:17.240
on the Google Infrastructure.

00:27:17.240 --> 00:27:19.580
I discussed about
the services running

00:27:19.580 --> 00:27:23.420
on the Google Infrastructure--
on the GCP and Google Cloud

00:27:23.420 --> 00:27:24.480
Infrastructure.

00:27:24.480 --> 00:27:27.430
And there is two
solutions, one for each,

00:27:27.430 --> 00:27:30.830
for the services on the
Google Infrastructure,

00:27:30.830 --> 00:27:36.110
like GCS, like Spanner, use
something called Private Google

00:27:36.110 --> 00:27:37.370
Access.

00:27:37.370 --> 00:27:42.410
The services built on GCP, like
Cloud SQL, Redis, Memchached,

00:27:42.410 --> 00:27:49.040
or TPU, or GKE Private Master
are using Private Service

00:27:49.040 --> 00:27:49.790
Access.

00:27:49.790 --> 00:27:54.320
And Private Service Access
is using VPC peering.

00:27:54.320 --> 00:27:56.630
Private Google access
is using a proxy

00:27:56.630 --> 00:27:59.890
to separate the GCP
infrastructure and the Google

00:27:59.890 --> 00:28:02.700
Infrastructure.

00:28:02.700 --> 00:28:07.760
So we are now going to see how
NetApp has built their services

00:28:07.760 --> 00:28:11.270
using Private Service
Access and how

00:28:11.270 --> 00:28:16.820
they use VPC peering to give
a very low latency volume

00:28:16.820 --> 00:28:17.882
application.

00:28:28.490 --> 00:28:29.990
ALIM KARIM: Can you
guys hear me OK?

00:28:33.080 --> 00:28:34.393
Thanks.

00:28:34.393 --> 00:28:35.060
Thanks, Olivier.

00:28:38.800 --> 00:28:40.960
So just a quick
show of hands, who

00:28:40.960 --> 00:28:42.910
has heard of Cloud
Volumes and the solution

00:28:42.910 --> 00:28:46.950
that NetApp and Google
are building together?

00:28:46.950 --> 00:28:50.850
Marketing departments doing
something, so that's good.

00:28:50.850 --> 00:28:53.903
So just for a recap,
what is Cloud Volumes?

00:28:53.903 --> 00:28:54.570
What does it do?

00:28:54.570 --> 00:28:55.620
Who's interested in it?

00:28:55.620 --> 00:28:58.830
I'll just spend one
slide covering that.

00:28:58.830 --> 00:29:01.950
Essentially, Cloud Volumes is a
fully managed cloud native file

00:29:01.950 --> 00:29:03.480
services offering.

00:29:03.480 --> 00:29:07.110
It's really meant to provide
high throughput, low latency

00:29:07.110 --> 00:29:09.720
NFS and SMB storage.

00:29:09.720 --> 00:29:12.990
And it's really targeted at
moving enterprise applications

00:29:12.990 --> 00:29:17.580
that customers used to
run on premise into GCP.

00:29:17.580 --> 00:29:20.610
So really used for
things like SAP,

00:29:20.610 --> 00:29:22.950
or running other
enterprise applications

00:29:22.950 --> 00:29:26.430
like content management
systems, B2B e-commerce systems,

00:29:26.430 --> 00:29:28.360
and such.

00:29:28.360 --> 00:29:30.730
Typically, what you'll
find is there's also

00:29:30.730 --> 00:29:33.910
a set of industries, like
media and entertainment,

00:29:33.910 --> 00:29:37.930
like life sciences, as well
as genomic sequencing, that

00:29:37.930 --> 00:29:41.830
are interested in running NAS
specific applications in GCP,

00:29:41.830 --> 00:29:43.630
and these are
basically industries

00:29:43.630 --> 00:29:45.880
that have been using NAS
on premise for a very, very

00:29:45.880 --> 00:29:48.880
long time and, therefore,
have tools and processes

00:29:48.880 --> 00:29:51.040
and skill sets built around NAS.

00:29:51.040 --> 00:29:53.560
And they want to extend
these into public cloud GCP

00:29:53.560 --> 00:29:54.850
more specifically.

00:29:54.850 --> 00:29:57.970
And therefore, they need
managed NAS solutions in GCP

00:29:57.970 --> 00:30:01.630
to move these business
processes or applications over.

00:30:01.630 --> 00:30:05.020
In terms of who's interested in
a solution like Cloud Volumes,

00:30:05.020 --> 00:30:08.015
typically it is cloud
architects or application owners

00:30:08.015 --> 00:30:09.640
who have been tasked
with, essentially,

00:30:09.640 --> 00:30:13.720
either moving a business process
or an application into GCP.

00:30:13.720 --> 00:30:15.370
And ultimately, it's
application teams

00:30:15.370 --> 00:30:19.405
being able to then provision
and manage these NFC or SMB end

00:30:19.405 --> 00:30:22.120
points themselves
once they're in GCP.

00:30:22.120 --> 00:30:24.700
So on the right hand side
is a quick screenshot

00:30:24.700 --> 00:30:26.560
of what this looks like.

00:30:26.560 --> 00:30:29.470
Basically, it's a native
interface into GCP

00:30:29.470 --> 00:30:31.060
through the Cloud Console.

00:30:31.060 --> 00:30:33.160
Customers can go in
and create a volume

00:30:33.160 --> 00:30:37.070
and essentially consume it from
either GCE or GKE instances.

00:30:39.720 --> 00:30:43.170
So one quick slide here to show
what the architecture of Cloud

00:30:43.170 --> 00:30:44.590
Volumes looks like.

00:30:44.590 --> 00:30:47.550
There's a dotted line in sort of
the middle of the picture that

00:30:47.550 --> 00:30:50.630
separates the control
plane from the data plane.

00:30:50.630 --> 00:30:52.380
I'll talk about the
control plane quickly.

00:30:52.380 --> 00:30:55.680
As I said, the users enter
into Cloud Volume service

00:30:55.680 --> 00:30:58.470
through their normal pathways,
either through the Cloud

00:30:58.470 --> 00:31:03.120
Console or using the API
and, soon coming, the CLI.

00:31:03.120 --> 00:31:05.280
That then hits our
control plane, which

00:31:05.280 --> 00:31:07.890
is running natively in GKE.

00:31:07.890 --> 00:31:11.850
And that talks to other
parts of the GCP ecosystem

00:31:11.850 --> 00:31:15.570
around authentication
for IAM, for the users

00:31:15.570 --> 00:31:16.890
that are logging in.

00:31:16.890 --> 00:31:19.920
Pushing metrics into
Stackdriver, logging metrics

00:31:19.920 --> 00:31:22.740
for auditing, as
well as integration

00:31:22.740 --> 00:31:23.740
into the billing APIs.

00:31:23.740 --> 00:31:25.380
And essentially, from
this perspective,

00:31:25.380 --> 00:31:29.760
the customer sees a native
volume in their GCP project.

00:31:29.760 --> 00:31:32.190
Volumes are isolated by project.

00:31:32.190 --> 00:31:34.230
And then these resources
are built directly

00:31:34.230 --> 00:31:36.660
into the customer's
billing account

00:31:36.660 --> 00:31:39.210
without having any
interaction with NetApp.

00:31:39.210 --> 00:31:41.910
The interesting
part that pertains

00:31:41.910 --> 00:31:45.670
to VPC peering and such is the
data path setup at the bottom.

00:31:45.670 --> 00:31:47.910
So for every customer
that comes in

00:31:47.910 --> 00:31:51.660
and every customer that has
a host project, basically,

00:31:51.660 --> 00:31:56.760
we set up a VPC peer connection
between their VPC and ours,

00:31:56.760 --> 00:31:58.710
where we provision all
of the resources that

00:31:58.710 --> 00:32:02.450
are needed to supply all of the
NFC end points that they need.

00:32:02.450 --> 00:32:03.950
So at the bottom
of the diagram, you

00:32:03.950 --> 00:32:06.450
see there is a set
of Cloud Volumes

00:32:06.450 --> 00:32:08.940
that this customer has
gone ahead and created.

00:32:08.940 --> 00:32:13.020
These volumes exist on the
producer side, so on our side,

00:32:13.020 --> 00:32:16.170
and then we expose mount
points, which are RFC

00:32:16.170 --> 00:32:20.190
1918 addresses that they give us
back into their infrastructure

00:32:20.190 --> 00:32:22.640
so that they can mount
these NFS volumes

00:32:22.640 --> 00:32:25.275
and essentially access them
from either GCE or GKE.

00:32:28.510 --> 00:32:31.360
So what does private services
connectivity do for us?

00:32:31.360 --> 00:32:33.080
Really, it's three key things.

00:32:33.080 --> 00:32:35.440
Number one is we really want
to simplify the end user

00:32:35.440 --> 00:32:38.210
experience around that
data path setup, right.

00:32:38.210 --> 00:32:41.010
So we just want
customers to come in

00:32:41.010 --> 00:32:43.690
and simply peer with us
and our producer services

00:32:43.690 --> 00:32:45.922
to be able to get access.

00:32:45.922 --> 00:32:48.130
We want to really abstract
away all of the networking

00:32:48.130 --> 00:32:52.060
complexities that it takes to
deliver a service from an NFS

00:32:52.060 --> 00:32:54.160
perspective and keep
that on our side

00:32:54.160 --> 00:32:59.080
and simply expose the other end
of a VPC peer to the customer.

00:32:59.080 --> 00:33:01.990
With NFC applications
that I mentioned, like SAP

00:33:01.990 --> 00:33:04.690
and some of these other
industry specific applications,

00:33:04.690 --> 00:33:07.850
performance is a very,
very key criteria.

00:33:07.850 --> 00:33:10.060
Most applications need
a lot of throughput

00:33:10.060 --> 00:33:11.470
and very, very low latency.

00:33:11.470 --> 00:33:14.480
And VPC pairing allows
us to achieve that.

00:33:14.480 --> 00:33:16.750
And then, finally, it
gives our customers

00:33:16.750 --> 00:33:19.600
a uniform way of accessing
these services, just

00:33:19.600 --> 00:33:23.920
like other services like Cloud
SQL that Olivier talked about.

00:33:23.920 --> 00:33:28.260
The second big benefit is it's
a private IP based service.

00:33:28.260 --> 00:33:30.390
For any of you that
are familiar with NFS,

00:33:30.390 --> 00:33:32.920
you realize that the
data that is stored

00:33:32.920 --> 00:33:36.520
on NFS and the applications
that use NFS typically

00:33:36.520 --> 00:33:40.480
contain sensitive,
secure data, which

00:33:40.480 --> 00:33:43.690
means that you can't really
have public endpoints on an NFS

00:33:43.690 --> 00:33:44.350
share.

00:33:44.350 --> 00:33:46.660
So this essentially
allows customers

00:33:46.660 --> 00:33:50.410
to give us their RFC 1918
addresses that they want

00:33:50.410 --> 00:33:52.640
to see on these end points.

00:33:52.640 --> 00:33:55.690
And then, finally, dealing with
large enterprise customers.

00:33:55.690 --> 00:33:59.420
Over 90% of them have
a shared VPC topology.

00:33:59.420 --> 00:34:02.320
So being able to support that
is a key requirement for us.

00:34:02.320 --> 00:34:04.300
And again, private
services connectivity

00:34:04.300 --> 00:34:07.060
gives us that, as well.

00:34:07.060 --> 00:34:08.620
And as Olivier
mentioned, so this

00:34:08.620 --> 00:34:11.889
allows us to have a one time
setup from a host project

00:34:11.889 --> 00:34:14.020
perspective into our service.

00:34:14.020 --> 00:34:16.360
And then all of the service
projects that follow

00:34:16.360 --> 00:34:17.920
can then just consume
these services

00:34:17.920 --> 00:34:20.049
without requiring
any additional setup.

00:34:20.049 --> 00:34:22.799
[MUSIC PLAYING]

